<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-1-27">
  <title>Resources for 1/27</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Describe what kinds of statements can be proven by <em>induction</em>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the steps necessary to prove a theorem by induction.  In particular, be able to explain the base case and the inductive step.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove theorems by induction.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-weak-induction">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Proof by Induction</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>proving theorems by induction</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />

    <problem>
      <title>Proof by Induction</title>
      <p>
        Use induction to prove the following.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>1+r+r^2+r^3+ \ldots + r^n = \frac{r^{n+1}-1}{r-1}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{1}{1 \cdot 2}+ \frac{1}{2 \cdot 3}+\frac{1}{3 \cdot 4}+\ldots + \frac{1}{n(n+1)}=\frac{n}{n+1}</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Section 4.1 (Sunstrom): pp. 169-180
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>